///1SYSVIEW
SYSVIEW.HLP - RETRO86 EMULATOR INFORMATION

Select a topic below. Press Return to move up one level.
From CP/M: HELP SYSVIEW

SYSVIEW 1.0 is a new CP/M-86 utility for the RETRO86 emulator.
It requests a snapshot from the emulator instead of guessing IBM
hardware from CPU flags, ROM addresses or BIOS equipment memory.

Run: SYSVIEW
Refresh: R
Exit: Q or Esc
More information: Space or Return at the paging prompt.

The matching emulator firmware update is required. Replacing only
the CP/M program does not add the new emulator information service.

///2INSTALL
INSTALL THE MATCHING FIRMWARE AND PROGRAM

Build and install the RETRO86 firmware that includes cpm86SystemInfo.
Copy the new SYSVIEW.CMD and SYSVIEW.HLP to your CP/M-86 disk.
The source folders systemDsk and utilities contain matching copies.
A running disk image also needs its program files updated, or must
be rebuilt from those source folders using your normal disk workflow.

Start at the CP/M prompt:
  SYSVIEW
Read the help with:
  HELP SYSVIEW

Use a VT100/ANSI terminal with at least 80 columns and 24 rows.
No configuration file is required by SYSVIEW itself.
Actual mounted images are determined by the emulator's configuration.

///2KEYS
NAVIGATION, REFRESH AND EXIT

At the end of the report:
  R or r       Request a new snapshot and display it from the start.
  Q or q       Return to CP/M.
  Esc          Return to CP/M.

At the -- More -- prompt:
  Space        Continue to the next page.
  Return       Continue to the next page.
  Q or Esc     Exit immediately.

Commands are single keystrokes; do not add Return after R or Q.
The utility does not require arrow keys or IBM keyboard codes.
The terminal's cursor is made visible on normal exit.

///2REPORT
WHAT THE REPORT CONTAINS

CP/M-side values:
  CP/M BDOS version   The version word returned by BDOS, hexadecimal.
  Current CP/M drive  Drive selected when the snapshot is displayed.
  User area          Current CP/M user number.

Emulator-side values:
  Firmware           Actual running application's version string.
  CPU core           tiny8086, the emulator implementation in use.
  Guest RAM          Memory successfully allocated for the guest.
  Host PSRAM         Installed PSRAM capacity of the emulator host.
  Host uptime        Elapsed host time since boot, in seconds.
  Guest instructions Core instruction counter since its last reset.
  Console/devices    Description of the emulated machine interface.
  Virtual disks      Actual mounted images, capacity and access mode.

Refresh obtains a new snapshot; the displayed values are not a live
continuously updating monitor.

///2CPU
CPU IDENTIFICATION

The CPU description comes from the emulator, not from a guest test
of reserved FLAGS bits. The current implementation is tiny8086.

A previous generic SYSVIEW could classify emulated FLAGS behaviour
as 80386 or later. That was not a reliable identification of this
emulator's instruction set. This version does not make that guess.

The description identifies the guest emulator core, not the physical
CPU of a Mac and not the ESP32's native instruction set.
No CPU-speed measurement or instruction-by-instruction capability
probe is performed by SYSVIEW.

///2MEMORY
GUEST RAM AND HOST PSRAM

Guest RAM is the size successfully allocated to cpm86Core.
For the current configuration it is normally 640 KiB.
It is not the amount of free memory available to a CP/M application:
CP/M itself, the loaded program and other allocations occupy space.

Host PSRAM is the total installed external RAM reported by the ESP32
runtime. It is not free PSRAM and is not all assigned to CP/M-86.
The report deliberately distinguishes host storage from guest RAM.

Values are taken from the running emulator rather than from a
possibly absent IBM BIOS data area at segment 0040h.
One KiB is 1024 bytes.

///2DISKS
MOUNTED VIRTUAL DISKS

The emulator reports all six virtual slots, A through F.
For a mounted slot the report includes:
  drive letter
  exact image size in bytes and approximate size in KiB
  read-only or read/write access
  actual host-side image pathname used for that mount

An unmounted slot is explicitly shown as not mounted.
The final count is mounted slots, not a claim about physical drives.

A long image pathname wraps onto indented continuation lines.
Control characters and non-ASCII pathname bytes are replaced with ?
so image names cannot inject terminal escape sequences.

The access mode is the mode of the opened image. It does not override
CP/M file attributes or guarantee that every disk operation succeeds.
SYSVIEW does not open, change, select or write guest disk sectors.

///2DEVICES
WHY THE REPORT DOES NOT INVENT PC HARDWARE

The RETRO86 CP/M-86 machine is a custom emulator interface.
It does not provide the IBM hardware inventory expected by the old
SYSVIEW. This program therefore describes the actual interface:
  console byte stream with an ANSI terminal expected
  no IBM video-adaptor or IBM ROM interface
  no FPU or game port provided by this machine interface
  CP/M LIST redirected to the console, rather than a physical LPT

There is no invented ROM manufacture date or VGA identification.
Host firmware version and uptime replace misleading BIOS guesses.
The details describe this machine interface, not all ESP32 hardware
or every possible future peripheral extension.

///2UPTIME
UPTIME AND INSTRUCTION COUNTER

Host uptime is derived from the host's monotonic timer.
It counts seconds since host boot, not wall-clock date or time,
and not necessarily the duration of the current CP/M session.

The instruction counter belongs to the guest CPU core and counts
instructions since that core's last reset. It includes CP/M and
utility execution, not just your application instructions.

Both are captured when a snapshot is requested.
The report does not calculate a CPU clock rate or benchmark score.
Refreshing the screen itself executes additional guest instructions.

///2COMPATIBILITY
OLD FIRMWARE AND OTHER EMULATORS

SYSVIEW probes for the information interface before requesting data.
If it is absent, the display explains that the matching RETRO86
firmware update must be installed first.

The current older RETRO86 port handler returns FFh for absent ports
and can print a one-time unavailable-device notice. The program then
shows the missing-interface message rather than reading random data.

This utility is intended for RETRO86, not as a universal IBM PC or
CP/M-86 hardware detector. Other emulators may treat unknown ports
differently and are not supported by this interface.

An invalid protocol header is rejected, and stream reading is bounded
to avoid an endless report if the interface malfunctions.

///2PROTOCOL
RETRO86-SYSINFO/1 - DEVELOPER INTERFACE

I/O ports, all transfers one byte:
  IN  F8h       Returns A6h when the service is available.
  OUT F8h,01h   Capture a snapshot and rewind the report stream.
  IN  F9h       Read the next report byte; 00h means end of stream.

Repeated reads after end return 00h.
The first report line is the exact protocol header:
  RETRO86-SYSINFO/1
followed by CR/LF. SYSVIEW validates this header before displaying
report contents. These ports are separate from the existing RETRO86
console, disk and timer ports.

The snapshot is read-only diagnostic information. Querying it does
not alter disk-transfer registers or reset the instruction counter.
Text capacity is 4096 bytes; current mount paths are bounded by the
emulator's normal drive-configuration limits.

///2TROUBLE
COMMON QUESTIONS

Most values are still missing.
  Check that both the firmware and SYSVIEW.CMD are the new versions.
  The new report starts with SYSVIEW 1.0 and has no IBM ROM date field.

It says the interface is unavailable.
  The running firmware does not offer the new F8h/F9h interface.
  Replacing a source file on your Mac does not update a flashed device.

The disk source folder has the new program but CP/M runs the old one.
  Update the mounted disk image too. Source folders are not live
  directories inside an already-created CP/M disk image.

The disk count differs from my expected physical floppy count.
  The report lists mounted virtual slots, not physical PC floppies.

The PSRAM number is larger than Guest RAM.
  Only part of the host's installed RAM is allocated to the guest.

The report pauses.
  Press Space or Return at -- More -- to continue, or Q to exit.

///2BUILD
REBUILDING AND VALIDATION

Guest source and builder are in assets/cpm86/SYSVIEW:
  python3 BUILD.py /path/to/SYSVIEW.CMD
The guest builder requires NASM. Using the supplied executable does
not require an assembler on the CP/M disk.

Firmware sources are cpm86SystemInfo.c/.h and the cpm86Machine adapter.
The cpm86Sysview host test executes the actual SYSVIEW.CMD on the
project's tiny8086 core and checks snapshot display, paging, refresh,
missing firmware, invalid headers and terminal restoration.

The cpm86Conformance CPU tests also pass with this change.
The guest integration test uses controlled host snapshots and BDOS
responses; a physical ESP32 device has not been flashed in this task.

///2ORIGINAL
HISTORICAL SYSVIEW.DOC

The following original text is retained for reference. It describes
Kirk Lawrence's IBM-compatible SYSVIEW 0.1, not the new independently
implemented RETRO86 information utility. Its historical web address
and original hardware claims are reproduced without updating them.

SYSVIEW  Version 0.1  [For IBM-Compatibles]
System Hardware Overview
Freeware from Kirk Lawrence

SYSVIEW gives you a quick "inside" look at an IBM-compatible computer.  It's
handy for scoping out unknown systems, or for confirming the hardware in a
known computer.

SYSVIEW identifies, and reports on the screen, various aspects of a computer's
hardware content.

SYSVIEW is a "work-in-progress," and future updates can be expected.
Please report any bugs through The CP/M-86 Software Repository web site
(http://www.seanet.com/~klaw)

SYSVIEW runs on =any= IBM-compatible computer.  It is fully compatible with
every type of video system...from MDA monochrome upwards.

SYSVIEW.CMD is free.  As is always the case with "freeware," there are no
guarantees whatsoever.  If you use the program, you do so entirely at your
own risk and peril.  Your use of SYSVIEW.CMD shall be prima facie evidence
under law that you understand and accept these terms and conditions.
